  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Living on the Edge: Managing Human-Jaguar Conflict in Unprotected Landscapes Surrounding the Proposed San Lucas Protected Area</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The jaguar (Panthera onca) is threatened throughout its range by illegal hunting of the species and its prey, and habitat destruction. These threats are decreasing jaguar populations and viable corridors necessary for the genetic viability and future survival of the jaguar, whose presence indicates habitat suitability for a myriad of species and ecological processes necessary to support sustainable livelihoods in rural areas. The purpose of this project is to reduce human-wildlife conflict and build local capacity through community depredation response teams to co-manage predator impact on livestock (GRECO: Grupo de Respuesta al Conflicto) in the Magdalena Medio region of Colombia. This project will support the proposed San Lucas Conservation Mosaic in northern Colombia, the only current gene flow corridor for jaguars between Central and South American jaguar populations, by reducing conflicts between ranchers and carnivores in the proposed mixed-use land. Specific activities include implementation of five demonstration ranches with proven anti-predation techniques, monitoring of the applied anti-predation techniques, and implementing a community GRECO to apply anti-predation best practices and techniques with ranchers in the area.</narrative>
  </description>
